<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Francis Younghusband</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1863">1863</a>–<a href="/years/1942">1942</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Sir Francis Younghusband</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Francis Younghusband was a British army officer and explorer of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a>. In 1903 and 1904 he led the British expedition to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a>, which killed hundreds of poorly armed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibetans">Tibetans</a> at Chumik Shenko, reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lhasa">Lhasa</a> and imposed a treaty opening Tibet to British trade. He later became president of the Royal Geographical Society and promoted the early Everest expeditions.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/francis-younghusband.jpg" alt="Francis Younghusband" width="320" height="506" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Lewis, Clifton · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Francis_Younghusband_1905.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
